    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Playing Soulstone Sanctuary on turn 1 instead of a colored mana source is suboptimal. The hand contains two 1-drop creatures (Burnout Bashtronaut and Hired Claw) that require red mana to cast. Soulstone Sanctuary only produces colorless mana (its creature activation costs 4 generic), so playing it turn 1 means no creature can be deployed this turn. Playing Mountain or Rockface Village would allow casting Burnout Bashtronaut or Hired Claw immediately, developing the board and starting to pressure the opponent. Rockface Village is arguably the best choice since it can produce red for creatures and has the pump activation for Lizards (relevant with two Hired Claws in hand).",
      "action_taken": "Played Soulstone Sanctuary as the turn 1 land drop",
      "better_line": "Play Rockface Village (or Mountain) and cast Burnout Bashtronaut or Hired Claw on turn 1 to begin applying pressure",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 3,
      "player": "Qwen3L Coach",
      "decision_index": 3,
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "During the opponent's end step (combat phase end on their turn 1), Gem3P Stoic declined to cast Opt, which is a pure-upside instant that could be cast at end of opponent's turn with no downside. Casting Opt here would use the available mana (Spirebluff Canal produces U or R) to scry 1 and draw a card, improving card selection and hand quality at no cost since the mana would otherwise go unused. There's no reason to hold the mana open — Spell Snare can't counter anything meaningful on turn 1 with the opponent having only 1 mana, and there are no other productive uses for the mana.",
      "action_taken": "Passed without casting any instant during the opponent's turn.",
      "better_line": "Cast Opt using Spirebluff Canal to scry 1 and draw a card, making use of otherwise wasted mana and improving card selection for future turns.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 9,
      "player": "Gem3P Stoic",
      "decision_index": 6,
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Gem3P Stoic passed the turn with mana available and Opt in hand. Opt is an instant that cantrips (scry 1, draw a card) with pure upside. There's no reason not to cast it at end of opponent's turn to dig deeper into the deck and improve card quality. Passing without using available mana on a cantrip wastes tempo. While Opt could technically be cast on the opponent's end step, the decision here was to pass entirely without casting it, and the game log shows the turn moved to the opponent without Opt being cast.",
      "action_taken": "Passed the turn without casting any spells, leaving 1 mana unused.",
      "better_line": "Cast Opt (either now or hold priority to cast on opponent's end step) to scry 1 and draw a card, using the available mana productively.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 10,
      "player": "Gem3P Stoic",
      "decision_index": 6,
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Playing Mountain instead of Rockface Village is slightly suboptimal. The hand contains two Hired Claws (Lizards), and Rockface Village can both produce red mana for creatures and later activate its ability to give a Lizard +1/+0 and haste. Since the deck is creature-heavy with Lizards, Rockface Village provides strictly more utility than a basic Mountain in this context. The creature mana restriction on Rockface Village's red production is largely irrelevant here since most of the hand is creatures anyway.",
      "action_taken": "Played a basic Mountain as the second land.",
      "better_line": "Play Rockface Village instead, preserving the basic Mountains for later turns when unrestricted red mana might matter more, while gaining access to the Lizard pump/haste ability for the Hired Claws.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 11,
      "player": "Qwen3L Coach",
      "decision_index": 8,
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Playing a creature in the precombat main phase when holding combat tricks or pump abilities and facing an opponent with open blue mana (Spirebluff Canal) is generally suboptimal. More importantly, Burnout Bashtronaut with menace is arguably the better turn 2 play here - it has menace making it harder to block, and its speed mechanic rewards early aggression. Since the opponent has only one land and likely can't block effectively anyway, the menace creature that grows with the speed mechanic and can eventually gain double strike is a stronger aggressive play than a 1/2 that needs other Lizards to trigger its ping ability. Additionally, casting a creature before playing the Rockface Village land drop is a sequencing error - playing Rockface Village first would give the option to use its haste-granting ability on a Lizard creature, though in this case the creature is being cast with the Mountain mana so it doesn't affect available mana this turn.",
      "action_taken": "Cast Hired Claw before playing land for turn and chose Hired Claw over Burnout Bashtronaut.",
      "better_line": "Play Rockface Village first (land drop), then cast Burnout Bashtronaut using Mountain - the menace creature is a stronger aggressive one-drop, and playing land before spells is correct sequencing.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 12,
      "player": "Qwen3L Coach",
      "decision_index": 8,
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Gem3P Stoic declined to cast Opt at the end of their opponent's turn or during their own precombat main phase. With Spirebluff Canal untapped providing blue mana, casting Opt would scry 1 and draw a card for just {U}, helping find a second land drop (they have Multiversal Passage and Spirebluff Canal in hand but need to develop). While holding up Spell Snare is reasonable, the opponent's lands are both tapped, so there's no threat coming this turn cycle. Casting Opt now helps dig toward lands and action. That said, they could also be planning to play a land and hold up Spell Snare on the opponent's turn 2 play — but the opponent already took their turn 2 (it's now Gem3P's turn 2), so holding Spell Snare mana is for the opponent's turn 3. They could cast Opt now, play Spirebluff Canal (their second land from hand), and still have Spell Snare mana up. Actually, looking more carefully: they have one Spirebluff Canal on the battlefield. They could play Multiversal Passage as their land for turn, cast Opt with Spirebluff Canal, and still have Multiversal Passage untapped — but Passage only adds one color based on choice. They could choose Island and have UU open for two Spell Snares, or cast Opt and have one Spell Snare up. Given the opponent has Hired Claw (a 1/2 that can grow), holding Spell Snare for a 2-mana spell is reasonable, but casting Opt to find lands/answers while still having Spell Snare available after playing their land seems strictly better than doing nothing.",
      "action_taken": "Declined to cast any spell, passing without using available mana.",
      "better_line": "Play Multiversal Passage (choosing Island), cast Opt with Spirebluff Canal to dig for answers/lands, still have Spell Snare available with the untapped Passage. Or at minimum, cast Opt now before playing land to see what's on top and inform the land play decision.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 14,
      "player": "Gem3P Stoic",
      "decision_index": 11,
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "During the opponent's combat step (or end of turn), Gem3P Stoic has open mana on Spirebluff Canal and could cast Opt to draw a card and scry 1 at instant speed, using mana that would otherwise go unused. This is a pure-value play at end of opponent's turn that costs nothing strategically - they have no reason to hold up the single blue mana since they're not in a position to cast Spell Snare (which counters MV 2 spells, and opponent's lands are tapped). Declining to Opt here wastes mana and misses a free card draw opportunity.",
      "action_taken": "Passed without casting any instant.",
      "better_line": "Cast Opt using the untapped Spirebluff Canal to scry 1 and draw a card, maximizing mana efficiency.",
      "type": "blunder",
